<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    package="com.rkskmp">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
        F-14 hardening: legacy shared-storage permissions are scoped to the API
        range where they actually grant something. Publishing them unbounded
        surfaces a scary "modify or delete your storage" grant in the OS
        permission list on modern devices, and gets flagged by the Play
        Console pre-launch report as an unjustified sensitive permission
        even though on API 30+ the OS silently ignores them.

        Attack surface being closed:
          * API 19-28: WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE grants write access to the
            ENTIRE `/sdcard/*` (every other app's shared files, DCIM,
            Downloads, Pictures, etc.) — enormously broader than the app's
            single "save downloaded PDF to Downloads" use case.
          * API 29+ : WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is a no-op for user files but
            still appears on the permission grant screen, degrading trust.
          * API 33+ : READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is superseded by the per-media
            READ_MEDIA_* permissions and has no effect.

        Why `tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion"` is REQUIRED:
        Two transitive React Native libraries declare these permissions in
        their own AndroidManifest.xml WITHOUT a maxSdkVersion:
            node_modules/react-native-blob-util/android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
                <uses-permission WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE />
                <uses-permission READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE />
            node_modules/react-native-fs/android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
                <uses-permission WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE />
        Without the marker, AGP's manifest merger keeps the library
        declaration (un-bounded) and drops our maxSdkVersion, silently
        defeating the fix. `tools:replace` forces our attribute to win.
        See https://developer.android.com/build/manage-manifests#markers

        Functional impact:
          * downloadFile() in src/utils/helper.js uses ReactNativeBlobUtil
            with `useDownloadManager: true`, i.e. it delegates writes to
            Android's system DownloadManager service. From API 29+ that
            service places files in public Downloads WITHOUT requiring
            the app to hold WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE.
          * All app-internal file I/O (RNFS.stat on picker output,
            image / video compression cache) targets app-private paths
            (getExternalFilesDir / cacheDir), which never require these
            permissions on API 19+.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="28"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />

    <!--
        F-14 hardening: modern per-media read permissions (API 33+).

        These MUST be declared because src/route/index.js requests them at
        runtime for API 33+ devices:

            Platform.Version >= 33
              ? [ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION, ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION,
                 READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE,       // scoped to <=32 by manifest
                 READ_MEDIA_IMAGES,           // declared below
                 READ_MEDIA_VIDEO,            // declared below
                 CAMERA]

        Prior to F-14 both READ_MEDIA_* were commented out of the manifest,
        so on Android 13+ devices the runtime `PermissionsAndroid.request(
        READ_MEDIA_IMAGES)` call was silently auto-denied by the OS (an
        app cannot request a runtime permission it did not declare) and
        image-picker gallery reads relied on the fallback ACTION_GET_CONTENT
        path.

        READ_MEDIA_AUDIO is intentionally NOT declared — the app has no
        audio-file read use case, and adding it would enlarge the grant
        surface without justification.
    -->
    <!-- <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_VIDEO" /> -->

    <!--
        Background-sync stack. RKSK ships an offline-first workflow for
        health workers — forms captured without connectivity are queued
        and uploaded later, and master-data / awareness videos are
        pulled down for offline use. That work runs inside
        `com.asterinet.react.bgactions.RNBackgroundActionsTask`, a
        foreground service declared below with foregroundServiceType
        "dataSync" (see the <service> block for the Play-policy
        justification of that specific type).

          - FOREGROUND_SERVICE        umbrella permission for any
                                      Service.startForeground(...) call.
          - FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC   Android 14+ (API 34) requires a
                                      permission that matches the
                                      declared foregroundServiceType, or
                                      the service is refused at start
                                      time. We use `dataSync` because
                                      the workload is user-initiated
                                      long-running data transfer
                                      (offline forms up, master data
                                      down). See android/PERMISSIONS.md.
          - WAKE_LOCK                 Required so the CPU isn't parked
                                      mid-upload while the phone screen
                                      is off. RN's default HTTP client
                                      uses OkHttp connect/read timeouts
                                      that DO fire when the CPU sleeps,
                                      producing spurious sync failures
                                      without this permission.
          - POST_NOTIFICATIONS        API 33+ (Android 13) runtime
                                      permission for the persistent
                                      ongoing-sync notification the
                                      foreground service must show. The
                                      OS enforces this even for
                                      framework-visible foreground-
                                      service notifications.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        F-23 hardening: strip over-broad transitive permissions that RN
        modules request but this app has no business holding.

        Each removal below targets a permission that:
          (a) is NOT declared by the app manifest itself,
          (b) is injected by a third-party AAR that RKSK depends on
              transitively, and
          (c) has NO code path in the app that actually exercises the
              functionality it grants (verified against src/ during
              F-23 investigation).

        The manifest-merger `tools:node="remove"` directive identifies
        <uses-permission> nodes by their android:name and drops any
        library-injected sibling with the same identifier from the
        final merged manifest. The pattern is identical to F-19's
        <uses-library> strip.

        If a future edit legitimately needs one of these permissions,
        DELETE the corresponding block below AND file the
        justification in android/PERMISSIONS.md. Do not silently add
        back — the release-build Gradle guard in
        android/app/build.gradle also asserts these stay absent from
        the merged output.

        Individual justification for each strip:

          1. DOWNLOAD_WITHOUT_NOTIFICATION (from react-native-blob-util)
             lets the app trigger `DownloadManager.request(...)` with
             the download progress hidden from the user. This is the
             textbook silent-exfiltration capability. RKSK's only
             DownloadManager caller is downloadFile() in
             src/utils/helper.js, which calls
             `ReactNativeBlobUtil.config({ useDownloadManager: true })`
             WITHOUT `notification: false`, so the user always sees a
             download-progress notification. The permission is dead
             weight AND red-flagged by Play Console pre-launch
             scanners.

          2. RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED (from androidx.work:work-runtime,
             pulled in by react-native-maps) allows a BroadcastReceiver
             to fire on device boot. WorkManager needs this ONLY on
             API < 23 to reschedule pending jobs after reboot; on
             API ≥ 23 it delegates to JobScheduler which persists
             work items across reboots natively. RKSK's minSdkVersion
             is 24, so the permission is unreachable on every runtime
             the app supports. Retaining it would let any code path
             (or supply-chain attacker) register a boot receiver that
             starts the app at power-on with no user gesture — a
             textbook auto-start persistence primitive.

          3. USE_BIOMETRIC (from react-native-keychain) is required
             only if the app uses `ACCESS_CONTROL.BIOMETRY_*` /
             `BIOMETRY_ANY_OR_DEVICE_PASSCODE` when calling
             Keychain.setInternetCredentials(...) or
             Keychain.setGenericPassword(...). RKSK's Keychain wrapper
             at src/utils/security/secureToken.js and
             src/utils/security/encryptionKey.js was intentionally
             simplified during F-04 to a passphrase-free / no-
             access-control configuration (see the F-04 changelog),
             so the biometric surface is unused. If a future edit
             adds biometric-gated storage, restore this permission
             AND update android/PERMISSIONS.md with the new access
             pattern.

          4. USE_FINGERPRINT (also from react-native-keychain) is the
             pre-API-28 counterpart of USE_BIOMETRIC. Deprecated by
             Android in API 28 (Pie). Even if biometric is
             reintroduced later, USE_FINGERPRINT should stay off —
             the BiometricPrompt API (available on our minSdk=24
             via the AndroidX shim) works with just USE_BIOMETRIC.

        See android/PERMISSIONS.md for the app-wide inventory.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.DOWNLOAD_WITHOUT_NOTIFICATION"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.USE_BIOMETRIC"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.USE_FINGERPRINT"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
        F-22 hardening: block BOTH backup / restore channels on all API
        levels the app runs on.

          - android:allowBackup="false" gates the pre-API-31 world (cloud
            backup AND the legacy device-transfer path both honour it).
          - android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
            gates API 31+. From Android 12 onward, allowBackup="false"
            still stops cloud backup but the setup-wizard "Copy from old
            phone" flow is governed by dataExtractionRules — without
            explicit <device-transfer> exclusions, all app-private data
            (files/, databases/, shared_prefs/, external/) copies across
            during D2D restore.

        Both attributes are declared explicitly so that:
          1. Legacy runtime enforcement (API 24-30) continues to work
             via allowBackup, and
          2. Modern runtime enforcement (API 31+) uses the XML rule set,
             which the OS reads for both cloud AND device-transfer
             decisions.

        See android/DATA_EXTRACTION.md for the threat model, the exact
        rule contents, and verification recipes.
    -->
    <application
      android:name=".MainApplication"
      android:label="@string/app_name"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
      android:allowBackup="false"
      tools:replace="android:allowBackup"
      android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
      android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
      android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
      android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
      android:supportsRtl="true">

      <!--
          F-19 hardening: strip the vestigial org.apache.http.legacy declaration.

          `com.google.android.gms:play-services-maps:19.1.0` (transitively pulled
          in by react-native-maps) injects the following into its own AAR's
          AndroidManifest.xml at line 33:

              <uses-library android:name="org.apache.http.legacy"
                            android:required="false" />

          The manifest merger then propagates it into the app's final manifest
          under <application>. That declaration exists purely as a compat
          safety-net for consumers on very old Play Services versions that
          still had code paths reaching `org.apache.http.HttpClient`; Maps SDK
          runtime has been on OkHttp / HttpURLConnection since v18+ and does
          not actually reach into the legacy library.

          Leaving it in has real cost:
             1. `org.apache.http.legacy.jar` shipped by AOSP is Apache
                HttpClient 4.x code frozen circa 2013–2015 and has multiple
                unpatched CVEs (CVE-2014-3577 hostname bypass, CVE-2015-5262
                TLS MitM, CVE-2020-13956 URI parsing) that will never be
                back-ported because the artefact is unmaintained.
             2. It signals to security scanners (MobSF, AppSweep, Play Console
                pre-launch) that legacy HTTP is available to the app.
             3. It expands the trusted classpath — any dependency, or an
                attacker who lands reflective code, can invoke
                `org.apache.http.*` classes with weak TLS defaults.

          RKSK's own Java/Kotlin surface and every react-native module we ship
          are verified free of `org.apache.http.*` imports (see
          android/APACHE_HTTP_LEGACY.md §Verification). We therefore instruct
          the manifest merger to REMOVE the play-services-maps-injected node
          via `tools:node="remove"`. The `android:name` value must match the
          library's declaration verbatim — the merger identifies uses-library
          nodes by their `android:name` and the removal only fires on an
          exact match.

          If a future dependency legitimately needs Apache HTTP, we must first
          replace it with a maintained client (OkHttp, ktor, Retrofit),
          not restore this node.
      -->
      <uses-library
          android:name="org.apache.http.legacy"
          android:required="false"
          tools:node="remove" />

      <!--
          F-25 hardening: strip DUMP-protected diagnostic / profileinstaller
          receivers that AndroidX transitive dependencies inject into the
          merged manifest as android:exported="true".

          THE VULNERABILITY

          `androidx.work:work-runtime:2.9.1` (pulled in transitively by
          react-native-maps) and `androidx.profileinstaller:profileinstaller:
          1.4.1` (pulled in transitively by the AGP baseline-profile
          machinery) each register a BroadcastReceiver whose manifest
          entry declares:

              android:exported="true"
              android:permission="android.permission.DUMP"

          The intent is "only privileged tools can trigger this receiver."
          But `android.permission.DUMP` has protectionLevel

              signature|privileged|development

          The `development` flag means the permission CAN be granted at
          runtime to non-system apps via `adb shell pm grant`. On any
          device with ADB debugging enabled, or on a rooted device, an
          attacker can obtain DUMP and then invoke either receiver.

          What the receivers do when invoked:

            * DiagnosticsReceiver (action `androidx.work.diagnostics.
              REQUEST_DIAGNOSTICS`) — WorkManager writes its internal
              scheduler state to logcat: pending Worker class names,
              constraints, initial delays, and back-off history. That
              leaks the app's autolinked background workflow shape to
              anyone with logcat access.

            * ProfileInstallReceiver (actions `INSTALL_PROFILE`,
              `SKIP_FILE`, `SAVE_PROFILE`, `BENCHMARK_OPERATION`) —
              triggers baseline-profile install / clear / save /
              benchmark operations. `SAVE_PROFILE` is particularly
              interesting to an attacker: it dumps the current
              runtime-captured hot-method profile to disk, which is
              essentially a call-graph fingerprint of the current
              session.

          THREAT MODEL

          Realistic exploitation vectors for RKSK:
            1. A malicious app on a rooted / dev-mode field device
               grants itself DUMP via ADB and triggers either receiver
               to enumerate WorkManager task classes or capture a
               runtime profile.
            2. A physical-access attacker with an unlocked bootloader
               / Frida installation triggers the receivers via `adb
               shell am broadcast` before the app is even foregrounded.
            3. Play Console pre-launch scanners (MobSF, Ostorlab,
               NowSecure) flag `exported="true"` receivers as attack
               surface regardless of the guarding permission's level.

          Not exploitable via:
            * Zero-click network — the receivers are only invocable
              via local IPC (Intent broadcast), not remotely.
            * A normal / unprivileged Play Store app on a stock device
              — DUMP is not granted to standard apps.

          Overall severity: LOW-TO-MEDIUM. But the remediation cost is
          also negligible (one `tools:node="remove"` per receiver), so
          the fix is worth it as defense-in-depth against dev-mode /
          rooted-device exploitation, and to close the corresponding
          pre-launch report finding.

          WHY STRIP RATHER THAN DOWNGRADE TO exported="false"

          Both receivers are triggered by OUT-OF-PROCESS callers —
          `DiagnosticsReceiver` by dev-tool broadcasts, `ProfileInstall
          Receiver` by Play Store service or `am broadcast`. Nothing
          IN RKSK's own code path invokes either receiver. Forcing
          `exported="false"` would make them invocable only from
          in-process code — which is exactly zero call sites, so the
          net effect is identical to full removal. `tools:node="remove"`
          is more honest about the intent and removes the entire
          `<receiver>` block from the merged manifest, reducing the
          static-analysis attack surface as well as the runtime one.

          WHY THIS IS SAFE FOR RKSK SPECIFICALLY

            * DiagnosticsReceiver — pure dev tool. Zero production
              value. Removing it strictly removes attack surface.
            * ProfileInstallReceiver — enables Google Play to install
              baseline profiles at first launch for a cold-start
              improvement (~10-30% on some devices). RKSK ships NO
              user-authored baseline profile
              (`android/app/src/main/baseline-prof.txt` does not
              exist), so the merged profile only contains minor
              transitive contributions from AndroidX libraries. The
              practical cold-start regression is negligible for a
              health-worker app where users spend hours in the app
              per session after opening it once.

          If the product team ever ships a hand-authored baseline
          profile (via Macrobenchmark or AGP's profile-generation
          tasks) AND the resulting cold-start improvement is
          measurable, delete the ProfileInstallReceiver removal
          block below AND update android/RECEIVER_HYGIENE.md with
          the new rationale.

          The release-build Gradle guard in
          android/app/build.gradle §mergedManifestStripRegistry
          also asserts both receivers stay absent from the merged
          output.

          See android/RECEIVER_HYGIENE.md for the full threat model,
          DUMP protection-level analysis, and verification recipes.
      -->
      <receiver
          android:name="androidx.work.impl.diagnostics.DiagnosticsReceiver"
          tools:node="remove" />
      <receiver
          android:name="androidx.profileinstaller.ProfileInstallReceiver"
          tools:node="remove" />

      <!--
          F-23 hardening — foreground-service classification.

          `RNBackgroundActionsTask` is react-native-background-actions's
          bundled Service class. It hosts the sync loop implemented in
          src/utils/bgservices/backgroundService.js which:

            1. Uploads offline-captured form data (ATP, Peer Educator,
               Peer Brigade, Referral, Individual Referral) to the
               backend when connectivity returns.
            2. Downloads master data lists (peer educator list, brigade
               list, IEC material, awareness videos, referral catalog)
               so the app can operate offline.
            3. Refreshes the user profile from the backend.

          Every sync cycle updates a persistent notification with a
          human-readable description of the current step
          ("Syncing atp form data...", "Data sync completed ✅", etc.),
          so the workload is always user-visible on API 26+ where
          foreground-service notifications are non-dismissible.

          Google's Android 14+ (API 34) foreground-service policy
          requires the declared foregroundServiceType to match the
          actual work. Per developer.android.com/about/versions/14/
          changes/fgs-types-required and the Play Console policy
          summary, "dataSync" is defined as:

              "Data transfer operations, such as:
               - Uploading or downloading data over the network
               - Backup and restore operations
               - Import or export operations
               - Fetching data
               - Local file processing
               - Transferring data between a device and the cloud
                 over a network."

          RKSK's usage is a direct match for the "uploading/downloading
          data over the network" and "transferring data between a
          device and the cloud" subclauses — the workload is
          user-initiated (background sync only runs when the user is
          signed in and has captured offline data), operates over the
          network, and produces observable UI state. It is NOT
          location tracking, media playback, camera capture, or health
          monitoring, so the more specialised foreground-service types
          (`location`, `mediaPlayback`, `camera`, `microphone`,
          `health`) do not apply.

          Android 15 (API 35) introduces a 6-hour daily
          `dataSync`/`mediaProcessing` quota. RKSK's sync cycles are
          intermittent — the loop sleeps between passes and
          terminates once no work remains (see the
          `checkIfSyncPending()` -> `stopAllBackgroundServices()`
          path in backgroundService.js) — so we are well under the
          quota in practice. If future product changes turn the sync
          loop into a persistent poller, revisit this justification
          and consider switching to WorkManager's periodic
          `WorkRequest` which is exempt from the quota.

          The matching `<uses-permission
          android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />`
          declaration lives at the top of this manifest — API 34+
          refuses to start the service if the permission is missing
          for the declared type.
      -->
      <service
          android:name="com.asterinet.react.bgactions.RNBackgroundActionsTask"
          android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync" />

        <!--
            F-06 hardening: react-native-webview ships a FileProvider whose default
            @xml/file_provider_paths maps `<external-path path="."/>` — i.e. the
            entire shared external storage root. We override the meta-data via
            manifest merger (`tools:replace="android:resource"`) so this provider
            binds to our narrow @xml/rksk_webview_file_paths, which only exposes
            the app-private external files directory that RNCWebView actually
            writes captured media into.

            The authority (`${applicationId}.fileprovider`) is kept because the
            library's runtime code hardcodes `getPackageName() + ".fileprovider"`
            in RNCWebViewModuleImpl.getOutputUri(). The duplicate-authority half
            of F-06 is resolved by removing react-native-send-intent from the
            dependency tree, so no other library now competes for this authority.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="com.reactnativecommunity.webview.RNCWebViewFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true"
            tools:replace="android:authorities,android:exported,android:grantUriPermissions">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/rksk_webview_file_paths"
                tools:replace="android:resource" />
        </provider>

        <!--
            F-08 hardening: the Google Maps SDK for Android reads its API key
            from this meta-data at runtime, so the string MUST end up in the
            packaged APK. What we control is that the string never lives in
            source control.

            The value is injected at build time via manifestPlaceholders
            configured in android/app/build.gradle. Developers set
            GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY in ~/.gradle/gradle.properties (per-user,
            outside VCS); CI supplies it via ORG_GRADLE_PROJECT_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY.
            See android/SECRETS.md for setup and for the Cloud Console
            restrictions that keep the key useless if it is extracted from
            a released APK.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.geo.API_KEY"
            android:value="${GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY}" />
      <!--
          F-11 hardening: MainActivity previously carried a second intent-filter
          declaring ACTION_VIEW + CATEGORY_DEFAULT + CATEGORY_BROWSABLE with no
          <data> element. Because no scheme / host / mimeType was pinned, the
          filter matched *any* Intent.ACTION_VIEW dispatched without a URI —
          i.e. any app on the device could invoke:

              Intent i = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW);
              startActivity(i);

          and RKSK's MainActivity would appear as a resolution candidate
          (or be launched outright when no other handler matched). Combined
          with android:launchMode="singleTask", this widened the surface for:

            - task-hijack / task-affinity attacks (StrandHogg-style),
            - unwanted activity reordering during sensitive flows
              (JWT-carrying requests, camera capture, PDF viewing),
            - forced foregrounding by malicious co-installed apps.

          The filter was also broken as a *deep-link* handler because deep
          links require a `<data>` element (scheme + host + optional
          pathPrefix / pathPattern) to route from an https:// or custom-scheme
          URI to the app.

          Audit of the app confirmed the filter was dead code:
            - `MainActivity.kt` does not override onNewIntent() and never
              reads intent.getData().
            - The JS side has zero `Linking.getInitialURL()` /
              `Linking.addEventListener('url', …)` handlers (only outbound
              Linking.openURL() calls: settings deep-links, Play Store).
            - No Firebase Dynamic Links, no OAuth redirect callbacks, no
              custom URL scheme, no react-navigation `linking` config.

          The safe fix is therefore to remove the malformed filter entirely.
          The MAIN + LAUNCHER filter below is retained (it's how the OS
          launches the app icon), and android:exported="true" is REQUIRED
          for that — do NOT flip it to "false". `exported="true"` is safe
          precisely because MAIN + LAUNCHER is the only remaining filter.

          If in the FUTURE the app genuinely needs to consume a deep link
          (OTP magic link, share intent, OAuth callback, etc.), add a
          *separate* intent-filter with a fully-specified <data> block
          scoped to the exact scheme + host you own, e.g.:

              <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                  <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                  <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                  <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                  <data android:scheme="https"
                        android:host="rksk.nhmmp.gov.in"
                        android:pathPrefix="/app/" />
              </intent-filter>

          …plus host a Digital Asset Links `assetlinks.json` file on that
          domain so the OS verifies ownership (`autoVerify="true"`), and
          add matching JS-side Linking handlers to actually consume the URL
          instead of the current no-op. Never re-introduce a VIEW filter
          without a `<data>` element.
      -->
      <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|orientation|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|uiMode"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
        android:screenOrientation="portrait"
        android:exported="true">
        <intent-filter>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent-filter>
      </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
